\title{Ideal-Valued Obstructions for Compatible Spectra over Dedekind Domains}

\author{Tran Hoai Ngoc Nhan}

\address{Faculty of Information Technology, Vinh Long University of Technology and Engineering\\
73 Nguyen Hue, Long Chau Ward, Vinh Long 85118, Vietnam\\
\email{nhanthn@vlute.edu.vn}}

\maketitle

% The publication-history block is intentionally omitted at submission stage.
% The publisher will insert received/revised/accepted/published metadata.

\begin{abstract}
Let $R$ be a Dedekind domain with fraction field $K$, and let $Q$ be an $n\times n$ orthogonal matrix over $K$. Define $L_Q$ to be the set of $\lambda\in R^n$ for which $Q\operatorname{diag}(\lambda)Q^T$ has entries in $R$, and put $M_Q=R^n/L_Q$. We describe $M_Q$ as a finitely generated torsion module whose cyclic factors are defined by ideals of $R$. A global obstruction theorem recovers these ideals from Smith normal forms after localization and from determinantal ideals, i.e. ideals generated by minors, of the rank-one symmetric matrices determined by the columns of $Q$. Exact rank-two and rank-three identities yield explicit low obstruction layers, complete descriptions in dimensions three and four, and finite counting profiles that recover the obstruction module. For Householder reflections $Q_v=I_n-2vv^T/(v^Tv)$, with $\operatorname{char}K\ne2$, we determine the local and global obstruction, its zeroth Fitting ideal, and sharp cardinality bounds when residue quotients are finite. In the quotient of the ideal class group by the subgroup generated by primes above $2$, the attainable denominator classes form exactly the square subgroup. An explicit example over $\mathbb Z[\sqrt{-5}]$ produces a nonprincipal cyclic obstruction factor.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Dedekind domain; ideal class group; orthogonal matrix; Smith invariants; Householder reflection; obstruction module.}

\ccode{2020 Mathematics Subject Classification: 15A29 (primary); 13F05, 15B10, 15B33 (secondary)}


\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
Let $R$ be a Dedekind domain---a Noetherian integrally closed domain in which every nonzero prime ideal is maximal---with fraction field $K$ \cite[Definition~1.2.1]{Cohen2000}; we use the standard ideal-theoretic language of Neukirch \cite[Ch.~I, \S\S3,11]{Neukirch1999}. Fix an orthogonal matrix $Q\in M_n(K)$, so $Q^TQ=QQ^T=I_n$. For $\lambda\in R^n$, write $\operatorname{diag}(\lambda)$ for the diagonal matrix with diagonal entries $\lambda_i$, and set
\begin{equation}
L_Q:=\{\lambda\in R^n:Q\operatorname{diag}(\lambda)Q^T\in M_n(R)\},
\qquad M_Q:=R^n/L_Q.
\end{equation}
Thus $L_Q$ records the integral spectra compatible with the prescribed orthogonal eigenbasis and $M_Q$ measures the obstruction to compatibility. We also write $\mathbf d_Q:=\{a\in R:aQ\in M_n(R)\}$ for the common entrywise denominator ideal. Van Werde uses the same entrywise construction under the name \emph{level ideal} in the study of integral cospectral mates \cite[Eq.~(2.3) and Theorem~2.1]{VanWerdeCospectral2026}.

